{
  "body": "The passage leaves this person deliberately unnamed, and that silence is itself the point. Mawlana Jafar ibn Sulayman, writing in his letter السَّيْفُ الْأَقْطَعُ فِي إِبْطَالِ الْبِدَعِ to the Ismaili community in Yemen, uses the phrase "the one whom they claim as their master and the owner of their affair" as a sharp rhetorical jab — one that lands hardest when the audience already knows who is meant.

In the polemical language of the Fatimid دعوة, the phrase صاحب أمرهم — the master and owner of their affair — typically points to whoever a rival faction recognizes as its **legitimate authority**, most often the Abbasid caliph, the symbolic head of Sunni Islam during the Fatimid period. The communities Mawlana Jafar addresses in Yemen looked to that rival seat rather than to the Fatimid Imam.

The specific instance of henna dyeing he alludes to is not spelled out. The author trusts his readers to recall a known episode — circulating in the community's oral memory or sectarian literature — in which that unnamed figure had himself dyed his hands with henna in the manner of women. By invoking it without naming it, Mawlana Jafar shames the opponents by linking their practice to their own authority while avoiding giving that figure the weight of a direct mention.

The passage does not supply the name, does not cite a date or occasion, and was never meant to. The accusation stands as an open wound in the argument — felt most keenly by those inside the community who already knew what it referred to, and deliberately opaque to outsiders. Trying to pin a definitive name or incident to it, beyond what the text itself gives us, would be adding to the passage rather than reading it.",
  "etymology": [
    "السَّيْفُ الْأَقْطَعُ (the severing sword): from the root ق-ط-ع, meaning to cut, sever or interrupt completely. الأقطع is the intensive form — not merely a cutting sword but one that severs without remainder. The title frames the letter as a decisive blade against بدعة, innovation."
  ]
}
